# SOLID Principles in C#

The SOLID principles are a set of five foundational guidelines that help developers create software that is modular, testable, and easy to maintain. These principles encourage clean architecture, separation of concerns, and flexible design. Below is an extended explanation of each principle with full markdown formatting and code examples.

---

## 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
A class should have **one reason to change**. This means each class should focus on a single responsibility.

### Bad Example
```csharp
public class ReportService
{
    public void SaveReport(Report report)
    {
        // Save to database
    }

    public void Log(string message)
    {
        File.WriteAllText("log.txt", message);
    }
}
```
This class handles both persistence and logging.

### Good Example
```csharp
public class ReportService
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public ReportService(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public void SaveReport(Report report)
    {
        // Save to database
        _logger.Log("Report saved");
    }
}

public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
}

public class FileLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        File.WriteAllText("log.txt", message);
    }
}
```
Each class now has one responsibility.

---

## 2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Classes should be **open for extension** but **closed for modification**.

### Bad Example
```csharp
public class PaymentProcessor
{
    public void Process(string method)
    {
        if (method == "card")
        {
            // Card payment
        }
        else if (method == "paypal")
        {
            // PayPal payment
        }
    }
}
```
Adding new payment methods requires editing the same class.

### Good Example
```csharp
public interface IPaymentMethod
{
    void Pay(decimal amount);
}

public class CardPayment : IPaymentMethod
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Paid with card");
    }
}

public class PayPalPayment : IPaymentMethod
{
    public void Pay(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Paid with PayPal");
    }
}

public class PaymentProcessor
{
    public void Process(IPaymentMethod method, decimal amount)
    {
        method.Pay(amount);
    }
}
```
New payment types can be added without modifying `PaymentProcessor`.

---

## 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
Subclasses should be usable anywhere their base class is expected.

### Bad Example
```csharp
public class Bird
{
    public virtual void Fly()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Flying");
    }
}

public class Penguin : Bird
{
    public override void Fly()
    {
        throw new Exception("Penguins cannot fly");
    }
}
```
This violates LSP because `Penguin` breaks the expected behavior.

### Good Example
```csharp
public abstract class Bird
{
    public abstract void Move();
}

public class Sparrow : Bird
{
    public override void Move()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Flying");
    }
}

public class Penguin : Bird
{
    public override void Move()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Walking");
    }
}
```
Now both subclasses behave correctly.

---

## 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.

### Bad Example
```csharp
public interface IAnimal
{
    void Walk();
    void Fly();
    void Swim();
}
```
Not all animals can fly or swim.

### Good Example
```csharp
public interface IWalk
{
    void Walk();
}

public interface IFly
{
    void Fly();
}

public interface ISwim
{
    void Swim();
}

public class Duck : IWalk, IFly, ISwim
{
    public void Walk() {}
    public void Fly() {}
    public void Swim() {}
}

public class Dog : IWalk
{
    public void Walk() {}
}
```
Interfaces are now small and focused.

---

## 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
High‑level modules should not depend on low‑level modules. Both should depend on abstractions.

### Bad Example
```csharp
public class EmailService
{
    private readonly SmtpClient _client = new SmtpClient();

    public void Send(string message)
    {
        _client.Send(message);
    }
}
```
This tightly couples `EmailService` to `SmtpClient`.

### Good Example
```csharp
public interface IEmailClient
{
    void Send(string message);
}

public class SmtpEmailClient : IEmailClient
{
    public void Send(string message)
    {
        // SMTP send logic
    }
}

public class EmailService
{
    private readonly IEmailClient _client;

    public EmailService(IEmailClient client)
    {
        _client = client;
    }

    public void Send(string message)
    {
        _client.Send(message);
    }
}
```
Now `EmailService` depends on an abstraction.

---

## Summary
The SOLID principles help developers build software that is flexible, maintainable, and resilient to change. By applying these principles, you reduce coupling, improve readability, and create systems that scale gracefully.
